Biology and its branches
Biology comes from the Greek bios (life) and logos (study). It asks how living things are built, how they work, where they came from, and how they interact. Because life is so broad, biology splits into branches. Zoology studies animals; botany studies plants; microbiology studies organisms too small to see, such as bacteria; genetics studies inheritance; ecology studies how organisms relate to each other and their environment; anatomy studies body structures; and physiology studies how those structures function. Modern biology connects all of these: a doctor treating malaria uses microbiology (the Plasmodium parasite), ecology (mosquito breeding sites), genetics (drug resistance) and physiology (how the human body responds). Like all sciences, biology works through observation, hypotheses, controlled experiments and peer-reviewed evidence — not opinion. Throughout this course you will practise thinking like a biologist: observe carefully, measure where possible, and change your mind when the evidence demands it.

The seven characteristics of living things
Biologists recognise seven characteristics shared by all known living things. A useful memory aid is MRS GREN: Movement (all living things can move — animals walk, plants turn leaves toward light, cells stream their cytoplasm); Respiration (releasing energy from food, aerobically or anaerobically); Sensitivity (detecting and responding to stimuli, such as a seedling bending toward light); Growth (permanent increase in size through cell division); Reproduction (producing offspring, sexually or asexually); Excretion (removing toxic waste products of metabolism, such as carbon dioxide and urea); and Nutrition (taking in and using food for energy and building materials). No single characteristic proves life — a car moves and uses fuel but does not grow, reproduce or excrete. An object is considered living only when it shows ALL seven. This strict rule is why viruses are usually called non-living: outside a host cell they show none of the seven, even though they carry genetic material.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 1, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Define biology and name its major branches","List the seven characteristics of living things","Decide whether borderline cases (viruses, seeds, fire) are alive, with reasons"],"introduction":"Biology is the scientific study of life — from a single bacterial cell to a whole rainforest. Before we study cells, genes and ecosystems, we must answer one question precisely: what does it mean to be alive?","key_points":["MRS GREN lists the seven characteristics of life","Evidence, not appearance, decides borderline cases","Biology advances through testable, repeatable evidence"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 1, The Study of Life (themes and characteristics of living things)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod01:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = 