From atoms to ecosystems
Living things are organised in a hierarchy, and each level is built from the one below: atoms join into molecules (such as water, proteins and DNA); molecules assemble into organelles (tiny cell structures like the nucleus and mitochondria); organelles work inside cells, the basic units of life; similar cells group into tissues (muscle tissue, leaf epidermis); tissues form organs (heart, leaf, root); organs cooperate in organ systems (circulatory system, shoot system); systems make an organism (a goat, a maize plant); organisms of one species form a population; populations of different species sharing a habitat form a community; and the community plus its non-living environment (soil, water, air, sunlight) forms an ecosystem. Learn this chain in order: atom, molecule, organelle, cell, tissue, organ, system, organism, population, community, ecosystem.

Emergent properties
Each level of organisation shows emergent properties — new behaviours that the parts alone do not have. A single nerve cell cannot feel pain, but billions wired together produce sensation. One water molecule is not wet; trillions together flow and dissolve. Emergence is why biologists study every level: you cannot fully explain a fever by staring at one white blood cell, nor predict a fishpond's harvest from one tilapia. Reduction (studying parts) and systems thinking (studying wholes) must work together. This idea returns throughout the course — for example, consciousness emerging from brain cells, or an epidemic emerging from individual infections.$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 2, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Order the levels of organisation from atom to ecosystem","Explain why new properties emerge at each level","Place familiar examples (blood, leaf, pond) at the correct level"],"introduction":"A single atom of carbon is not alive — yet arranged with other atoms into cells, it becomes part of a living mango tree. Organisation is what turns chemistry into biology.","key_points":["Order the eleven levels without hesitation","Cells are the basic units of life","Emergence explains why wholes exceed their parts"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 1, The Study of Life (levels of organisation)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod01:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
