Why classify?
Classification groups organisms by shared features so that knowledge transfers: learn how one mammal's heart works and you understand all mammals' hearts approximately. It reveals evolutionary relationships — lions and house cats share the order Carnivora because they share a recent ancestor. It also makes communication precise: the two-part scientific name (binomial nomenclature) gives every species exactly one global identity. Humans are Homo sapiens everywhere on Earth — no confusion from the thousands of local names. Names are written in italics (or underlined by hand), with the genus capitalised and the species lowercase: Mangifera indica (mango), Plasmodium falciparum (the malaria parasite).

The five kingdoms
The five-kingdom system sorts life by cell type, cell number and nutrition. (1) Monera (bacteria): prokaryotic, single-celled; some make food by photosynthesis or chemosynthesis, others absorb it — examples: Escherichia coli, Rhizobium in bean root nodules. (2) Protista: eukaryotic, mostly single-celled; plant-like (Euglena, diatoms), animal-like (Amoeba, Plasmodium) or fungus-like (slime moulds). (3) Fungi: eukaryotic, mostly multicellular; feed saprophytically by digesting dead matter externally — examples: mushroom, Rhizopus (bread mould), yeast. (4) Plantae: eukaryotic, multicellular, autotrophic (photosynthesis), with cell walls of cellulose — examples: maize, mango, mosses. (5) Animalia: eukaryotic, multicellular, heterotrophic (must eat), no cell walls, usually mobile — examples: goat, tilapia, earthworm, humans. Viruses belong to NO kingdom: they are particles of nucleic acid in protein, reproduce only inside host cells, and show no metabolism — the classic borderline case from Lesson 1.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 3, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Explain why classification is necessary","State the features of the five kingdoms with two examples each","Explain why viruses fit in none of the kingdoms"],"introduction":"Scientists have named about two million species, and millions more await discovery. Without a filing system, biology would be chaos — classification is that filing system.","key_points":["Five kingdoms and their distinguishing features","Binomial nomenclature rules","Viruses are acellular and kingdom-less"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapters 21–23 (viruses, prokaryotes and protists)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod01:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
