Organelles and their jobs
Both plant and animal cells share these parts. The cell membrane (a thin, partially permeable skin of lipid and protein) controls what enters and leaves. The cytoplasm is the jelly-like fluid where most reactions happen. The nucleus stores DNA — the instructions for building and running the cell — and controls cell activities; inside it, the nucleolus makes ribosomes. Mitochondria release energy from glucose during respiration, so active cells (muscle, root hair) contain many. Ribosomes build proteins from amino acids. The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a folded membrane network: rough ER (studded with ribosomes) folds new proteins, smooth ER makes lipids. The Golgi body receives, packages and ships proteins in vesicles. Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that break down waste (common in animal cells). Vacuoles store substances — animal cells have small temporary ones. Plant cells add three extras: a rigid cellulose cell wall for support, chloroplasts containing chlorophyll for photosynthesis, and one large permanent central vacuole that keeps the cell turgid with cell sap.

How to learn cells for examinations
Examiners test cells through labelled diagrams and function questions. Learn each organelle as a pair: STRUCTURE + FUNCTION + ADAPTATION. Example: mitochondrion — folded inner membrane (cristae) gives a large surface area for respiration enzymes; muscle cells pack in thousands. Exam traps to avoid: (1) the cell wall and cell membrane are different structures — do not swap them; (2) chloroplasts trap light energy, mitochondria release energy — opposite jobs; (3) the large permanent vacuole belongs to mature plant cells only. When drawing cells, use a sharp pencil, single (not sketchy) lines, no shading, and straight label lines that touch the structure — biological drawing rules are marked strictly in practical papers.$wdthseed$, NULL, 40, 1, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Label the organelles of plant and animal cells","State the function of each major organelle","Name the three structures found in plant but not animal cells"],"introduction":"Every organism you studied in Module 1 is built from cells. This lesson opens the cell and names its working parts — the vocabulary for the rest of biology.","key_points":["Plant-only trio: wall, chloroplasts, large vacuole","Mitochondria release energy; chloroplasts trap it","Draw cells with clean pencil lines and touching labels"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 4, Cell Structure (eukaryotic cells and organelles)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
