Two cell designs
Prokaryotic cells (bacteria and archaea) are small — typically 1 to 5 micrometres — with DNA in a single circular chromosome free in the cytoplasm (the nucleoid region, not a true nucleus), no membrane-bound organelles, small 70S ribosomes, and often a cell wall, slime capsule, plasmids (small DNA rings carrying bonus genes such as antibiotic resistance) and flagella for movement. Eukaryotic cells (plants, animals, fungi, protists) are larger — typically 10 to 100 micrometres — with DNA in linear chromosomes inside a membrane-bound nucleus, membrane-bound organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi), larger 80S ribosomes, and complex internal transport. The word roots help: pro = before (no nucleus), eu = true (true nucleus). Mitochondria and chloroplasts probably began as engulfed bacteria — the endosymbiotic theory — which is why they still carry their own small circular DNA.

Why cells are microscopic
As a cell grows, its volume (which demands food and produces waste) grows as the CUBE of its radius, while its surface area (which supplies and removes) grows only as the SQUARE. Double a cell's width and it needs 8x the supplies through only 4x the membrane — it starves. This surface-area-to-volume crisis forces cells to stay small, divide, or fold their membranes (root hairs, villi, cristae all do this). Bacteria exploit smallness brilliantly: huge surface area per volume means blistering-fast exchange, letting E. coli divide every 20 minutes in warm food — which is also why food poisoning strikes so fast.$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 2, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["State three differences between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells","Label a bacterial cell","Explain why cells stay microscopic (surface area to volume)"],"introduction":"A bacterium and a human cheek cell are both alive, but one is a studio apartment and the other a mansion. Size and internal organisation change everything about how a cell lives.","key_points":["Pro = no nucleus; eu = true nucleus","Surface area to volume limits cell size","I = M × A triangle for magnification"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 4 (prokaryotic vs eukaryotic cells), Chapter 22 (bacteria)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
