Diffusion
Diffusion is the net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration, down the concentration gradient, until evenly spread. It needs no energy from the cell — particle motion (kinetic energy) drives it. Four factors speed it up: (1) a steeper concentration gradient, (2) higher temperature (faster particles), (3) larger surface area, and (4) shorter distance (thinner membranes). Examples: oxygen diffusing from alveoli into blood, carbon dioxide diffusing out of leaf cells into air spaces, perfume spreading across a room. Once concentrations equalise, particles still move but there is no NET movement — dynamic equilibrium.

Osmosis
Osmosis is the net movement of WATER molecules from a region of higher water concentration (dilute solution) to a region of lower water concentration (concentrated solution) across a partially permeable membrane. Learn this definition word-perfect — examiners reject 'movement of water from high to low concentration' without mentioning the membrane. Consequences: animal cells in pure water swell and burst (lyse) because they have no wall; in concentrated solution they shrivel (crenate). Plant cells in pure water become turgid (firm — the vacuole pushes the membrane against the wall, supporting soft stems); in concentrated solution the membrane pulls away from the wall — plasmolysis — and the plant wilts. Root hairs absorb soil water by osmosis; kidney tubules reabsorb it.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 3, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Define diffusion and state four factors that speed it up","Define osmosis precisely","Predict whether cells gain or lose water in any solution"],"introduction":"Your lungs load oxygen and dump carbon dioxide without any pumping by you. Smell from the kitchen reaches your room with no fan. Both are diffusion — physics doing biology's delivery work for free.","key_points":["Osmosis definition must name the membrane","Turgid = firm plant cell; plasmolysed = membrane pulled away","Gradients, temperature, area and distance control diffusion rate"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 5, Structure and Function of Plasma Membranes"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
