Active transport
Active transport moves substances from lower to higher concentration — AGAINST the gradient — using energy from respiration (ATP) and carrier proteins in the membrane. It is the only transport process that needs the cell's own energy, so it stops when respiration is blocked (e.g. by cyanide or lack of oxygen). Examples: root hair cells pumping mineral ions (nitrates, phosphates) from dilute soil water; kidney tubule cells reabsorbing all glucose from the filtrate; gut villi absorbing glucose and amino acids into blood after digestion. Comparison table for examinations: diffusion — down gradient, no energy, small molecules/gases; osmosis — water only, down water gradient, needs membrane, no energy; active transport — against gradient, NEEDS energy and carriers, ions and nutrients.

Exchange surfaces
Multicellular life needs specialised exchange surfaces, all sharing four adaptations: LARGE surface area, THIN barrier (short diffusion distance), GOOD blood supply or ventilation (to maintain gradients), and MOIST surfaces (gases dissolve before diffusing). Root hairs: millions of tiny extensions giving enormous area for water/mineral uptake. Small-intestine villi: finger-like folds (with microvilli on top) absorbing digested food into dense capillaries. Alveoli: ~300 million air sacs with one-cell-thick walls wrapped in capillaries for gas exchange. Leaves: broad, thin, with stomata and air spaces for CO2 entry. Examination sentence pattern: NAME the adaptation → STATE the feature → EXPLAIN the effect ('Villi increase surface area, so more glucose diffuses into blood per minute').$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 4, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Contrast active transport with diffusion and osmosis","Give two examples of active transport in plants and animals","Explain how root hairs, villi and alveoli are adapted for exchange"],"introduction":"Roots absorb minerals from soil even when the soil holds LESS mineral than the root already has — swimming upstream against the gradient. That takes energy, and it is worth it.","key_points":["Only active transport uses the cell's ATP","Exchange surfaces: area, thinness, supply, moisture","Answer adaptations as feature → exchange effect"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 5 (bulk and active transport), Chapter 31 (plant nutrition)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod02:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.con