The carbohydrate family
Carbohydrates contain carbon, hydrogen and oxygen (roughly 1 C : 2 H : 1 O). Monosaccharides are single sugars: glucose (blood sugar, the fuel of respiration), fructose (fruit sugar) and galactose. Disaccharides join two monosaccharides with a glycosidic bond, releasing water (condensation): maltose (glucose + glucose), sucrose (glucose + fructose, table sugar), lactose (glucose + galactose, milk sugar). Polysaccharides chain hundreds together: starch (energy store in plants — amylose + amylopectin in yam, rice, cassava), glycogen (energy store in animals, packed in liver and muscles, more branched for fast release), and cellulose (structural walls of plant cells — long straight chains bundled into tough fibres). Digestion reverses building: hydrolysis ADDS water to split big molecules back into sugars your blood can carry.

Why structure fits function
Starch is ideal for storage: compact, coiled, insoluble (so it does not leak out of cells or distort osmosis) yet easily hydrolysed back to glucose on demand. Glycogen's extra branches give animals rapid glucose release during a sprint. Cellulose is built for strength: straight chains hydrogen-bonded into microfibrils with enormous tensile strength — a tree is a cellulose skyscraper. This pattern — structure determining function — repeats for every molecule in this module and is an examination favourite: always link a structural feature to the job it performs.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 1, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Distinguish monosaccharides, disaccharides and polysaccharides","State the roles of glucose, starch, glycogen and cellulose","Explain condensation and hydrolysis in plain words"],"introduction":"Every marathon runner's energy gel, every grain of rice, and the paper of your notebook are all carbohydrates — one family of molecules wearing many uniforms.","key_points":["Mono/di/poly = 1/2/many sugars","Condensation out, hydrolysis in (water)","Starch stores, glycogen sprints, cellulose builds"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 3, Biological Macromolecules (carbohydrates)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod03$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod03:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = ex