Triglycerides and phospholipids
A triglyceride joins ONE glycerol to THREE fatty acids by condensation (three ester bonds, three waters released). Fatty acids may be saturated (no double bonds — straight chains that pack tightly, solid at room temperature: butter, palm oil, animal fat) or unsaturated (one or more double bonds kink the chain so it cannot pack — liquid oils: groundnut, soya, olive oil). Roles of lipids: (1) concentrated energy store (9 kcal/g vs 4 for carbohydrate — seeds and adipose tissue); (2) insulation — blubber, subcutaneous fat and myelin sheaths slow heat loss and speed nerve impulses; (3) protection — fat cushions kidneys and eyes; (4) membranes — phospholipids (glycerol + 2 fatty acids + phosphate head) form the bilayer of every cell membrane, with waterproof cuticles and waxes protecting leaves and skin. Steroids (cholesterol, sex hormones) are lipids too, built from rings rather than fatty acids.$wdthseed$, NULL, 25, 2, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Describe triglyceride structure","State four roles of lipids","Explain why lipids store twice the energy of carbohydrates"],"introduction":"A groundnut is small but keeps you full for hours. Lipids pack more than twice the energy per gram of any sugar — and build every membrane in your body.","key_points":["Glycerol + 3 fatty acids = triglyceride","Double bonds kink chains → oils stay liquid","Fat = slow vault; glycogen = fast wallet"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 3 (lipids)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod03$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod03:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_ur