The active site
Enzymes are globular proteins that speed up (catalyse) specific reactions without being used up. Each enzyme has an ACTIVE SITE — a pocket whose shape fits its SUBSTRATE exactly. The lock-and-key model says the fit is rigid and perfect; the newer induced-fit model says the active site moulds slightly around the substrate, like a glove tightening on a hand — induced fit is the accepted refinement. After binding, the enzyme stresses bonds (or brings reactants together), products leave, and the enzyme is free again. Specificity is absolute: amylase digests starch but ignores protein; each enzyme fits one substrate type. The enzyme-substrate complex is temporary; the enzyme emerges unchanged and ready for the next reaction.

Factors affecting rate
Three graphs to master. (1) TEMPERATURE: rate rises with kinetic energy (more successful collisions) to an OPTIMUM (about 37°C for human enzymes), then CRASHES as heat denatures the enzyme — the curve is asymmetric (gentle rise, cliff fall). (2) pH: each enzyme has an optimum pH (pepsin ~pH 2 in the stomach; amylase ~pH 7) with rate falling both sides as charges change shape; extremes denature. (3) SUBSTRATE CONCENTRATION: rate rises then LEVELS OFF (plateau) when every active site is occupied — the enzyme is saturated; only adding more enzyme raises the plateau. Inhibitors: competitive blockers fit the active site (beaten by adding substrate); non-competitive binders distort the enzyme elsewhere (cannot be beaten by substrate). Many drugs and poisons (cyanide, snake venom components) are enzyme inhibitors.$wdthseed$, NULL, 40, 4, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Define enzymes and explain the active site","Compare lock-and-key and induced-fit models","Predict and explain rate curves for temperature, pH and substrate concentration"],"introduction":"Without enzymes, digesting lunch would take years instead of hours. These protein catalysts accelerate reactions millions of times — and this lesson's graphs appear in almost every biology examination.","key_points":["Active-site fit gives absolute specificity","Denaturation cliff makes the temperature curve asymmetric","Saturation plateaus are about occupied sites"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 6 (energy and enzymes)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod03$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod03:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded