Interphase and PMAT
The cell cycle is mostly INTERPHASE (growth + DNA replication): the cell grows (G1), copies every chromosome into two sister chromatids (S phase), then grows and prepares to divide (G2). Mitosis itself has four stages — remember PMAT. PROPHASE: chromosomes condense (become visible), the nuclear membrane breaks down, and spindle fibres form. METAPHASE: chromosomes line up singly along the cell equator, attached to spindle fibres at their centromeres. ANAPHASE: sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles (V-shapes trailing) — the defining moment of exact separation. TELOPHASE: nuclear membranes reform around each set, chromosomes uncoil. CYTOKINESIS then splits the cytoplasm: animal cells pinch inward (cleavage furrow); plant cells build a cell plate outward. Result: TWO genetically IDENTICAL diploid cells, each with the full chromosome number (46 in humans).

Why exactness matters
Every chromatid separation must be perfect: lose or gain one chromosome and the daughter cell usually dies or malfunctions (Down syndrome comes from an extra chromosome 21 after a separation error). Spindle checkpoints pause division until every chromosome is attached — the cell proofreads itself. When checkpoints fail and division runs wild, the result is a tumour: cancer is fundamentally a cell-cycle disease. This is why Module 4 matters beyond examinations — chemotherapy drugs target rapidly dividing cells precisely because they exploit mitosis.$wdthseed$, NULL, 40, 1, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["State what happens in interphase and each stage of mitosis","Identify PMAT stages from diagrams or micrographs","Explain why chromosome copies must separate exactly"],"introduction":"You began as one cell and now hold about 30 trillion. Every one came from mitosis — the most important dance in biology, performed identically billions of times a day.","key_points":["PMAT order and one diagnostic feature each","DNA replicates in S phase, before mitosis","Mitosis = identical diploid daughters"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 10, Cell Reproduction (the cell cycle, mitosis)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.cou