Two divisions, one copying
Meiosis starts like mitosis: DNA replicates ONCE in interphase. Then come TWO divisions. MEIOSIS I (reduction division): homologous chromosome PAIRS (one from each parent) line up together; in anaphase I, whole HOMOLOGUES separate (not chromatids) — this HALVES the number (46 → 23 in humans). MEIOSIS II: the two cells divide again like mitosis — chromatids separate — giving FOUR haploid cells. In males all four become sperm; in females usually ONE becomes the egg (the rest form polar bodies). Key vocabulary: DIPLOID (2n, paired chromosomes — body cells), HAPLOID (n, single set — gametes), HOMOLOGOUS PAIR (matching chromosomes, one maternal one paternal, same genes in the same order). Fertilisation (n + n) restores 2n — which is why children resemble BOTH parents.

Mitosis versus meiosis
Memorise this comparison — it is examined constantly. DIVISIONS: mitosis 1, meiosis 2. CELLS PRODUCED: 2 vs 4. CHROMOSOME NUMBER: stays diploid vs halved to haploid. GENETIC IDENTITY: identical copies vs all different. LOCATION: body cells (growth/repair) vs reproductive organs (gametes). SEPARATION EVENT: chromatids separate (anaphase) vs homologues separate first (anaphase I), then chromatids. Both begin with DNA replication and share PMAT-like machinery — meiosis is essentially 'mitosis twice with pairing first'. One-line summary: mitosis CLONES, meiosis SHUFFLES AND HALVES.$wdthseed$, NULL, 40, 3, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["State that meiosis produces four haploid, genetically different cells","Describe the key events of meiosis I and II simply","Explain why fertilisation restores the diploid number"],"introduction":"If sperm and eggs each carried 46 chromosomes, every generation would double the count until cells burst. Meiosis solves this by halving — with a creative bonus: no two gametes are ever alike.","key_points":["Diploid body cells, haploid gametes","Homologues separate in anaphase I","Count centromeres for chromosomes"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 11, Meiosis and Sexual Reproduction"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
