Two shuffles in meiosis
Variation source 1 — CROSSING OVER (prophase I): homologous chromosomes press together and swap matching segments, creating chromatids that mix maternal and paternal alleles — brand-new gene combinations on single chromosomes. Variation source 2 — INDEPENDENT ASSORTMENT (metaphase I): each homologous pair lines up randomly, maternal-left or maternal-right, INDEPENDENTLY of other pairs. With 23 pairs, humans produce 2^23 (over 8 MILLION) gamete combinations per person — before crossing over multiplies it further. Then RANDOM FERTILISATION picks one sperm from millions for one egg. Combined, the odds of two identical siblings (excluding identical twins, who split from ONE zygote) are effectively zero. Mutations add brand-new alleles occasionally — the ultimate source of all variation.

Why variation matters
Variation is evolution's raw material: when environments change (new disease, drought, antibiotic), SOME variants survive and breed — natural selection needs differences to select. Low-variation populations (cheetahs, cloned bananas) face extinction from single threats; high-variation ones adapt. This links Module 4 directly to Module 6 (evolution): meiosis GENERATES the differences that selection SORTS. Sexual reproduction's cost (finding mates, halving gene transmission) is repaid in adaptability — which is why nearly all complex life kept it despite the cost.$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 4, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Explain crossing over and independent assortment","Show why siblings (except identical twins) are genetically unique","Link variation to survival and evolution"],"introduction":"Your parents' genes could combine in trillions of ways — and you are exactly one of them. Meiosis is the shuffling machine behind your uniqueness.","key_points":["Crossing over = segment swaps","Assortment = 2^n combinations","Uniqueness needs all three shuffles"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 11 (variation from meiosis)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
